  "id": "chunk_6",
  "chunk": "Zork, originally developed at MIT and released in 1977 (later commercially\nreleased by Infocom [13] in 1980), represents a canonical example of interac-\ntive fiction that challenges players through pure textual interaction. The game\npresents players with descriptions of locations, objects, and situations entirely\nthrough natural language (e.g., “You are standing in an open field west of a\nwhite house, with a boarded front door”), and players respond by typing com-\nmands in natural language (e.g., “pick-up sword” or “go north”). Success in\nZorkrequires players to construct and continuously update mental models of\nspatial environments—imagining room layouts, remembering connections be-\ntween locations, and tracking inventory—without any visual aids. Players must\nmaintain a coherent state across long sequences of actions, recalling which puz-\nzles they have solved, which items they have collected, and how different objects\n2",
